[
  {
    "question_eid": 17592186064044,
    "math_academy_id": "e-3726",
    "kp_ids": [],
    "canonical_memberships": [
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186064162,
        ":knowledge-point/id": "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d",
        ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"
      }
    ],
    "old_fields": [],
    "difficulty": null,
    "problem_before": "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](images/08/0811362e235ec7c6e93a8bf892a451f8c347d0f633f251b9ed955062a53dd6c3.png)",
    "worked_solution_before": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true.",
    "learner_uses": [
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186729511,
        ":task-item/content": {
          ":db/id": 17592186064044
        },
        ":task-item/elapsed-seconds": 3.745,
        ":task-item/id": "0acdb315-1048-4803-86d4-f37352c10cf1",
        ":task-item/next": {
          ":db/id": 17592186729512
        },
        ":task-item/status": {
          ":db/ident": ":task-item.status/completed"
        }
      }
    ],
    "commit_status": "not_submitted",
    "decision": "retain_canonical",
    "reason": "Canonical worked example has no answer fields to convert."
  },
  {
    "question_eid": 17592186064116,
    "math_academy_id": "e-6169",
    "kp_ids": [],
    "canonical_memberships": [
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186064273,
        ":knowledge-point/id": "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674",
        ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"
      }
    ],
    "old_fields": [],
    "difficulty": null,
    "problem_before": "![](images/50/50581635273c185b9ca3aefa907b2e2a4981ec1bf6be1c687bec47591e126a89.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?",
    "worked_solution_before": "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](images/56/56e798c0942ddcf0b1786afb054ab6dfbea7768c86751555808d86cc4cfa89f6.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point.",
    "learner_uses": [
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186729421,
        ":task-item/content": {
          ":db/id": 17592186064116
        },
        ":task-item/elapsed-seconds": 8.829,
        ":task-item/id": "662533b8-06d3-4ff3-9e6f-a506fe1d0a46",
        ":task-item/next": {
          ":db/id": 17592186729422
        },
        ":task-item/status": {
          ":db/ident": ":task-item.status/completed"
        }
      }
    ],
    "commit_status": "not_submitted",
    "decision": "retain_canonical",
    "reason": "Canonical worked example has no answer fields to convert."
  },
