    "problem_before": "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
    "worked_solution_before": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\u201d is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of $f(x)$ has a has a sharp edge (cusp) at $x=2.$  Therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)$ does not exist and hence it can't be equal to zero.\n\n- Statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\u201d is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(x)\\le f(2)$ for $x$ near the point $x=2.$\n\n- Statement \u201c$f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\u201d is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=2$ since the derivative does not exist at this point.",
