    "problem_before": "![](images/5d/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{\u2032}(x)=0?$\n\n${g}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=${{field-1}} and $x\\,=${{field-2}}",
    "worked_solution_before": "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/36/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$",
    "learner_uses": [],
    "commit_status": "not_submitted",
    "decision": "retain_entry",
    "reason": "Existing numerical entry structure is not a finite-subset radio task."
  },
  {
    "question_eid": 17592186238003,
