    },
    "problem_before": "![](images/55/55d089ab7c7947e3f9d67a11fd69fa3903abd6f10ef09853fe018eaa16524ec1.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?",
    "worked_solution_before": "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=-5$ and $x=-2$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n![](images/69/6983f3615bf721529173c11de3a9218962a57e4be5247f4bda1ac2fa496531ee.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are all values in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=-2$ and $x=2$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points.",
    "learner_uses": [],
    "commit_status": "not_submitted",
    "decision": "retain_radio",
    "reason": "Whole intervals or interval unions are mutually exclusive descriptions of the requested set. Inventing a partition into selectable atoms would redesign the response rather than convert an existing finite list."
  },
  {
    "question_eid": 17592186238004,
    "math_academy_id": "q-103434",
    "kp_ids": [
