    "difficulty": {
      ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
    "problem_before": "![](images/ea/eab84ed04b86678fb4a8d23c010e9a22beead062595b49818ac95e996c3173e6.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?",
    "worked_solution_before": "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at any point between $x=3$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](images/ab/ab200bebf80bbd1abe6e0c43ec124fe359da25f38d4346d25d8741decc88aa26.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$, are all values in the open interval $(3,4).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=3$ and $x=4$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points. But they are not stationary points.",
    "learner_uses": [],
    "commit_status": "not_submitted",
    "decision": "retain_radio",
    "reason": "Whole intervals or interval unions are mutually exclusive descriptions of the requested set. Inventing a partition into selectable atoms would redesign the response rather than convert an existing finite list."
  },
