    "problem_before": "![](images/36/36477ec421ff4b7f349a060df762eadee83b008fbfc9e021a8893cc45e405cae.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-2$",
    "worked_solution_before": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2$ since $f(-2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough  to $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a sharp edge at $x=-2$ and therefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(-2)$ does not exist.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
    "learner_uses": [],
    "commit_status": "not_submitted",
    "decision": "convert",
    "objects": [
      {
        "source_label": "I",
        "content": "$f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$",
        "selected": true
      },
